      "notes": [
        "⚠️ **The past tense works the opposite way from the present.** The present changes by **gender & number** but ignores the person. The past does the reverse: it changes by **person** (I/you/he/we), and barely cares about gender — except for \"you\" and \"he/she\".",
        "**The marker is at the END of the word, not the front.** ־תִּי = I; ־תָּ / ־תְּ = you (m/f); no ending = he; ־ָה = she; ־נוּ = we; ־תֶּם = you (pl); ־וּ = they. Memorize this row once, then stick it onto any root.",
        "**Here Hebrew starts to feel like English *-ed*.** אָכַל (he ate) → אָכַלְתִּי (I ate). The root stays, the tail changes. Like *walk → walked* — except the Hebrew tail also tells you who did it.",
        "⚠️ **Hebrew has no separate \"did\" vs. \"have done\".** אָכַלְתִּי means \"I ate (yesterday)\", \"I've eaten\" and \"I had eaten\" all at once. The sentence decides. Good news: one form instead of three.",
        "**הָיָה = \"was, were\" — the past of \"to be\".** This verb isn't used in the present at all (remember: אֲנִי מוֹרֶה with no \"am\"), but it **must** appear in the past: הָיִיתִי עָיֵף = \"I was tired\". Leave out הָיָה and your sentence sounds like now, not yesterday.",
        "**הָיָה is also used for weather and states with no subject**: הָיָה חַם (it was hot), הָיָה נֶחְמָד (it was nice). You'll use these words a lot to answer \"how was it?\".",
        "⚠️ **Verbs with weak letters go their own way.** רָאָה (see) becomes רָאִיתִי, not רָאַתְתִּי; הָיָה becomes הָיִיתִי; שָׂחָה (swim) becomes שָׂחִיתִי. The group is small but full of common verbs. Don't memorize the rule — just learn to recognize the forms when you hear them.",
        "**צִלַּמְתִּי** belongs to the פִּעֵל verb family (whose present form starts with מְ־), and its past endings are **exactly the same**. The row of endings works for every verb family without exception — a huge saving."
      ],
